## Fix process — scaled by risk

Every fix gets a real analysis and a real review. How much of that has to be **written out in the conversation** depends on what the change touches. Choose the tier from the code touched, not the size of the diff: a one-line change to caching or authz is high-risk (one-line `view_as` and ETag changes have caused real bugs here). **When unsure, go one tier up.**

| Tier | Covers | What to write |
|---|---|---|
| **High-risk** | Authz, permissions, privileged-view · login, sessions, cookies, CSRF, MFA · schema, migrations, backfills, bulk data writes · caching (ETag, `max-age`, Redis keys) · billing and money · realtime fan-out, jobs, concurrency, multi-worker state · boot config and secrets · uploads and outbound requests (SSRF) | The four phases below, visible in the conversation, **one item at a time** |
| **Standard** | Ordinary logic, endpoints and UI behaviour outside the high-risk list | A note before (root cause with `file:line`; the change; callers touched; **one named failure mode** — what happens when the fix itself fails; how you will verify) and a review after (the checks run — the `AGENTS.md` working-standard set: bugs, dead code, unhandled paths, perf/security misses — plus the better-pass, then verdict). Related items in the same feature may share one note and one review if each item is named in both |
| **Trivial** | Copy and text, CSS or token tweaks, comments and docstrings, renames inside one file, an obvious one-liner that adds no branch or state | One line in your summary: what changed and where. No separate plan or review |

### High-risk: the four phases

**Phase 0 — Analyse.** Read the relevant source in full, then write: which files you read and what the code actually does (trace entry point → issue, `file:line` for key logic); the root cause, kept distinct from symptoms; the callers and invariants that depend on current behaviour; any pre-existing bug you found (see below). Verify every claim from an analysis doc against source — the doc is a starting point, not a spec.

**Phase 1 — Plan.** Write: the change and the files affected; edge cases and failure modes — per-request variants (session, `view_as`, cookies) that change the response but not the URL (caching / ETag suspects), concurrency and async ordering, RLS / GUC / connection state, and what happens when the fix itself fails; regression risk and which callers depend on current behaviour; how you will verify. If the implementation diverges from the plan, stop and re-plan.

**Phase 2 — Review.** Re-read the changed code in context (the surrounding function, imports, callers), not just the diff. State the result for each check that applies: type mismatches (UNION ALL column types, NULL casts, dataclass fields); cache correctness (does the response vary by session, cookie or `view_as` on the same URL? stale `max-age`?); async correctness (missing `await`, races); error paths; DOM and event lifecycle (work on detached nodes, listener leaks, closure vs dataset flags); dead code and misleading comments; a simpler approach or an existing helper. End with a verdict.

**Phase 3 — Fix findings.** Real findings are fixed before the item is marked done, and the fix gets its own Phase 2. Loop until the review is clean.

### Honest review

"No issues found" is a valid verdict when you say what you checked. Do not invent findings to look thorough. Do not rubber-stamp either: name the checks you ran, and skip a check that cannot apply (no cache, no DOM) with a word rather than pretending to run it.

### The review record's own fields (standard tier and above)

A review that reaches ≥2 implementation files carries, in its written record: `re-derived:` — the review re-derives the change from the code and names at least one element or decision (a paraphrase of the plan's goal line is not a re-derivation); findings carry a **severity class** — `defect:` / `absence:` / `quality:` / `hygiene:` / `approach:` (approach = the change works but a different approach should have been taken); `from-scratch:` — the cold-read verdict ("seeing this change with no context, would I sign it"); and a **`## Significant-improvement walk`** — at least three of `correctness-blast`, `perf-headroom`, `capability-vs-referent`, `simplification`, `consistency-drift`, each `found:` or `defended: <reason>` — "nothing significant to improve" is earned per axis, not declared once. Any field may be `n/a — <why>`; `review-thin: <why>` releases the whole set as an honest declaration — and the declaration stays in the record where the user can see it.

### Pre-existing bugs found along the way

1. State the bug with `file:line` and its failure mode.
2. **Fix it now only if** it is in the lines you are already changing, or it would break or undermine your fix.
3. Otherwise add it to the feature's task MD as an open item and list it in your final summary to the user. Do not widen the current slice to fix it.
4. Never bury it: a found bug is either fixed, or in a task MD and reported to the user.

### Anti-patterns

- Jumping to code from an issue title or an analysis doc without reading the source.
- Under-tiering: calling a caching, authz or data change "trivial" because the diff is small.
- Marking an item done because the import works — import success is not correctness.
- Implementing several high-risk items and writing one review at the end.
- Noting a real review finding and moving on without fixing it.
- Changing ETag or `max-age` without checking for per-request variants that share the URL (the most common source of caching bugs here).

